ion, where the response variable \\( Y \\) is expressed as a function of the factors \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\) and their interactions. The general model can be represented as:\n\\[\nY = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + \\beta_{12} X_1 X_2 + \\ldots + \\epsilon\n\\]\nwhere \\( \\beta \\) represents the coefficients, and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nFactorial designs can be structured as either full factorial or fractional factorial designs. A full factorial design examines all possible combinations of factor levels, while a fractional factorial design investigates a subset of these combinations, which is particularly useful when dealing with a large number of factors.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of the results obtained from factorial designs relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the residuals.\n3. Homogeneity of variances (homoscedasticity).\n4. Linearity of the relationship between factors and the response variable.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nFactorial designs are applicable when researchers aim to study the effects of two or more factors on a response variable, especially when interactions between factors are of interest. They are particularly useful in industrial experiments, agricultural studies, and any field where multiple variables may influence an outcome.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of factorial designs typically involves Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to determine the significance of the main effects and interactions. The steps include:\n1. Formulating the null and alternative hypotheses.\n2. Calculating the ANOVA table, including sums of squares, degrees of freedom, mean squares, and F-statistics.\n3. Conducting post-hoc tests if necessary to explore significant effects further.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nFactorial designs offer several advantages:\n- T